  "title": "5...f6, holding the pawn",
  "intro": "Black props up e5 with a pawn. It is the most common answer at club level and it is the one Fischer met most often, so it is the line to know cold.\n\n## Why Black plays it\n\nThe pawn on e5 is the only thing stopping White from taking over the centre, and a pawn is the cheapest defender there is. The cost is that f6 takes the square away from Black's own knight and puts a hole on e6 and g6 that a white knight would love.\n\n## Your answer\n\n6.d4 straight away. Black's centre pawn now has to decide, and every answer suits White. If Black takes, recapture with the knight and you have the healthy majority you came for. If Black leaves the tension, take on e5 yourself and the f-pawn has to recapture, which leaves Black with a weak pawn instead of a strong one.\n\n## The line to remember\n\n6.d4 exd4 7.Nxd4 c5 8.Nb3 Qxd1 9.Rxd1 and the queens are off with the structure fixed. Then Bf4 hits at c7, the knights go to c3 and d4, and White plays on the kingside where the healthy pawns are. Nothing here is sharp. It is a slow squeeze and it is exactly the game the Exchange was chosen to reach.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nTaking on e5 with the knight, which loses a piece to ...fxe5. Putting the knight on f5, where it is traded off and leaves White with a useless pawn there. Recapturing on f3 with the g-pawn if Black offers the trade. And forgetting the queen: after 8...Qxd1 the recapture is not optional.",
